import PageHeader from "../../components/shared/PageHeader.astro";
import P2PSubNav from "../../components/shared/P2PSubNav.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LeadBlock from "../../components/shared/LeadBlock.astro";
import ContactBanner from "../../components/shared/ContactBanner.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How P2P Media Loader Works: WebRTC P2P Video | Novage"
  canonical="p2p-media-loader/technical-overview"
  description="How P2P Media Loader builds a P2P video network: WebRTC, STUN and WebTorrent trackers, how peers share HLS and DASH segments, integrations, DRM and browsers."
>
  <Header />
  <div id="content">
    <article>
      <PageHeader title="P2P Media Loader" accent="Technical Overview">
        <P2PSubNav current="technical-overview" />
      </PageHeader>
      <div class="page container content">
        <h2>Key components of the P2P network</h2>
        <ImageBlock>
          <P2PNetworkDiagram slot="image" />
          <p>All the components of the P2P network are free and open-source.</p>
          <p>
            <strong>P2P Media Loader</strong> is an open-source JavaScript
            library that uses features of modern web browsers (HTML5 video and
            WebRTC) to deliver media over P2P and play it through integrations
            with popular HTML5 video players. It doesn't require any browser
            plugins or add-ons.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>P2P Media Loader</strong> requires these browser features:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>WebRTC Data Channels</strong> to exchange data between
              peers
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Media Source Extensions</strong> or{" "}
              <strong>Managed Media Source</strong>, required by HLS.js, Shaka
              Player and other MSE-based engines for media playback
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/STUN">STUN</a> server is
            used by{" "}
            <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebRTC_API">
              WebRTC
            </a>{" "}
            to gather{" "}
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interactive_Connectivity_Establishment">
              ICE
            </a>{" "}
            candidates. There are many running public servers on the{" "}
            <a href="https://gist.github.com/mondain/b0ec1cf5f60ae726202e">
              Public STUN server list
            </a>
            .
          </p>
          <p>
            A compatible <a href="https://webtorrent.io/">WebTorrent</a> tracker
            is used for WebRTC signaling and to create swarms of peers that
            download the same media stream. Public trackers are available at{" "}
            <code>wss://tracker.webtorrent.dev</code> and{" "}
            <code>wss://tracker.openwebtorrent.com</code>. You can run your own
            tracker with open-source implementations:{" "}
            <a href="/case-studies/wt-tracker">wt-tracker</a>,{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/greatest-ape/aquatic">Aquatic</a>,{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/OpenWebTorrent/openwebtorrent-tracker">
              OpenWebtorrent Tracker
            </a>
            ,{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/webtorrent/bittorrent-tracker">
              bittorrent-tracker
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </ImageBlock>

        <p>
          P2P Media Loader is configured to use public STUN and WebTorrent
          servers by default, so for simple use cases you don't need to run any
          server-side software. For production, we recommend running your own
          tracker.
        </p>

        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <p>
          A web browser runs a video player integrated with the P2P Media Loader
          library. Each instance of the library is called a{" "}
          <strong>peer</strong>, and many peers together form the P2P network.
        </p>
        <p>
          P2P Media Loader first downloads media segments over HTTP(S) from the
          source server or CDN, so playback starts quickly. If no peers are
          available, it keeps downloading over HTTP(S), just like a traditional
          media stream.
        </p>
        <p>
          It then sends media stream details and connection information (ICE
          candidates) to WebTorrent trackers, which return a list of other peers
          watching the same stream.
        </p>
        <p>
          P2P Media Loader connects to these peers, downloads media segments
          from them, and shares the segments it already has.
        </p>
        <p>
          Segments the swarm doesn't hold yet are downloaded over HTTP(S) and
          then distributed to others over P2P. Each such segment has one{" "}
          <strong>elected owner</strong>, chosen with{" "}
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rendezvous_hashing">
            rendezvous hashing
          </a>{" "}
          applied to a P2P mesh: every peer scores itself and its connected
          neighbors for that segment — not the whole swarm — and the lowest
          score fetches it. The decision is local, so it needs no coordination
          round trip and works even though no peer sees the entire swarm.
          Ownership rotates from segment to segment, so no peer becomes the
          swarm's permanent origin, and if an owner is late, the others cover
          for it before playback is at risk.
        </p>

        <h3>A manifest-driven core</h3>
        <p>
          Since version 5.0, the core parses HLS and MPEG-DASH manifests itself.
          A player integration hands over every manifest the player fetches,
          routes segment requests through the core, and reports playback state.
          Segment identity comes from the manifest — the HLS media sequence
          number or the DASH presentation time — so peers on different players
          derive the same identity for the same segment and share one swarm.
          DASH <code>SegmentBase</code> streams share too: the core reads the
          segment index from the <code>sidx</code> box in the response the
          player already fetched.
        </p>

        <h3>How much traffic goes over P2P</h3>
        <p>
          In ideal conditions each segment is downloaded over HTTP(S) once and
          then shared between peers, so for N viewers of a live stream about 1/N
          of the traffic goes over HTTP(S) and the rest over P2P:
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Viewers of a live stream</th>
              <th>HTTP(S)</th>
              <th>P2P (ideal conditions)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>2</td>
              <td>50%</td>
              <td>50%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>10</td>
              <td>10%</td>
              <td>90%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>20</td>
              <td>5%</td>
              <td>95%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>100</td>
              <td>1%</td>
              <td>99%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>1,000</td>
              <td>0.1%</td>
              <td>99.9%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          Version 5.0 brings live streams closer to this ideal. The
          rendezvous-hashing election stops peers from racing for their own
          copies of new segments, and placing the player deeper in the live
          window gives peers time to supply segments before the player asks.
        </p>
        <p>
          For video on demand, the ratio depends on how many viewers watch the
          same part of the video at the same time. In real-world conditions it
          also depends on network conditions and peer connectivity.
        </p>

        <h2>Integrations and packages</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://npmjs.com/package/p2p-media-loader-core">
              p2p-media-loader-core
            </a>{" "}
            — the core, with HLS and MPEG-DASH manifest parsers
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://npmjs.com/package/p2p-media-loader-hlsjs">
              p2p-media-loader-hlsjs
            </a>{" "}
            — HLS.js integration
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://npmjs.com/package/p2p-media-loader-shaka">
              p2p-media-loader-shaka
            </a>{" "}
            — Shaka Player integration
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://npmjs.com/package/p2p-media-loader-dashjs">
              p2p-media-loader-dashjs
            </a>{" "}
            — dash.js integration
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://npmjs.com/package/p2p-media-loader-videojs">
              p2p-media-loader-videojs
            </a>{" "}
            — Video.js 8 integration
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Video.js 10 needs no separate package: it plays through HLS.js and
          dash.js, which the existing integrations drive. Players such as
          Vidstack, Clappr, MediaElement, Plyr, DPlayer, OpenPlayerJS and
          PlayerJS work through the integration for the engine they are built
          on.
        </p>
        <p>
          All packages are available as ES modules on the jsDelivr CDN. The core
          comes in three builds — with both manifest parsers, HLS only, or DASH
          only — so a page carries only the parsers it uses. Every integration
          also ships an IIFE build for older browsers and Smart TVs.
        </p>

        <h2>DRM and encrypted streams</h2>
        <p>
          P2P Media Loader works with DRM-protected and encrypted streams,
          including Widevine, PlayReady, FairPlay and HLS AES-128:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Peers exchange segments exactly as the CDN delivers them, still
            encrypted. Decryption stays in the video player; the library never
            decrypts media and never stores or shares decrypted data.
          </li>
          <li>
            Key and license requests are never intercepted and never travel
            between peers — they always go directly to your license server.
          </li>
          <li>
            Not applicable to per-session encryption or forensic watermarking,
            where every viewer receives different bytes for the same segment.
            P2P must be disabled for such content.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Web browsers support</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Chrome on desktops and Android</li>
          <li>Firefox on desktops and Android</li>
          <li>Safari on macOS</li>
          <li>Safari on iPadOS (iPad)</li>
          <li>Safari on iOS (iPhone, iOS 17.1+ required)</li>
          <li>Edge on Windows</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          It also runs inside{" "}
          <a href="/blog/integrate-p2p-video-streaming-into-mobile-application">
            native iOS and Android apps
          </a>
          , including with native players.
        </p>

        <h2>Documentation</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Guides{" "}
            <a href="/blog/setting-up-p2p-video-on-a-web-page-in-5-minutes-for-free">
